xnu 0346: the GPU's clock can be moved on a running system, and the ring says
how much of a frame the GPU actually owns

The HFI power vote carries perf-table index 2 (285 MHz) because that is what
the bring-up sends when the GMU starts; kgsl sends a fresh one per submission,
so on Android the GPU runs at the OPP the load needs. Nothing raises it here,
and the measured frame cost is what a GPU at the idle OPP looks like.

`gpuperf N` (ring command) sets the index and re-sends the vote, so 285 MHz and
900 MHz can be compared in one boot instead of one boot each; `gpuperf` alone
prints the index in effect. The vote is sent after the CP has run, which is
where kgsl's does, and the ARC rails are already at their top level by then.

`gpustat` also prints `gpuacct`: submissions, dwords, how many fence waits the
clients did and how long they took, and the sum of doorbell-to-fence over the
whole session beside the wall clock it spans. That sum is GPU busy time: a
frame whose wait is the GPU drawing shows up as the same fraction of wall
clock, and a wait that is latency with an idle GPU does not.

